\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} F &= -\frac{GMm}{r^2} \end{align*}
where $G$ is the gravitational constant, $M$ is the mass of the Galaxy, and $m$ is the mass of the star. The centripetal force required to keep the star in circular orbit is given by
\begin{align*} F &= m\frac{v^2}{r} \end{align*}
Equating the two expressions for the force, we have
\begin{align*} m\frac{v^2}{r} &= -\frac{GMm}{r^2} \\ v^2 &= \frac{GM}{r} \end{align*}
Thus, the orbital velocity of a star at a distance $r$ from the center of the Galaxy is given by
\begin{align*} v(r) &= \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}} \end{align*}
The rotation curve of the Galaxy is the velocity of stars as a function of distance from the center of the Galaxy. Since the Galaxy is spherically symmetric, the rotation curve is the same at all distances from the center. Therefore, the rotation curve is given by
\begin{align*} v(r) &= \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}} \end{align*}
The rotation curve is a decreasing function of $r$, which means that stars orbiting at larger distances have lower velocities than stars orbiting at smaller distances.

